  1117 |         special tokens are NOT in the vocabulary, they are added to it (indexed starting from the last index of the
  1118 |         current vocabulary).
  1119 | 
  1120 |         When adding new tokens to the vocabulary, you should make sure to also resize the token embedding matrix of the
  1121 |         model so that its embedding matrix matches the tokenizer.
  1122 | 
  1123 |         In order to do that, please use the [`~PreTrainedModel.resize_token_embeddings`] method.
  1124 | 
  1125 |         Using `add_special_tokens` will ensure your special tokens can be used in several ways:
  1126 | 
  1127 |         - Special tokens can be skipped when decoding using `skip_special_tokens = True`.
  1128 |         - Special tokens are carefully handled by the tokenizer (they are never split), similar to `AddedTokens`.
  1129 |         - You can easily refer to special tokens using tokenizer class attributes like `tokenizer.cls_token`. This
  1130 |           makes it easy to develop model-agnostic training and fine-tuning scripts.
  1131 | 
  1132 |         When possible, special tokens are already registered for provided pretrained models (for instance
  1133 |         [`BertTokenizer`] `cls_token` is already registered to be `'[CLS]'` and XLM's one is also registered to be
  1134 |         `'</s>'`).
