   314 |             include `"inductor"` (default), `"aot_eager"`, `"cudagraphs"`. Backends vary by PyTorch
   315 |             version - see PyTorch docs for available options.
   316 |         torch_compile_mode (`str`, *optional*):
   317 |             Compilation mode for `torch.compile()`. If set, automatically enables `torch_compile`.
   318 |             Options: `"default"`, `"reduce-overhead"` (minimize Python overhead), `"max-autotune"`
   319 |             (aggressive optimization, slower compile time).
   320 | 
   321 |         > Kernels
   322 | 
   323 |         use_liger_kernel (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   324 |             Enable [Liger Kernel](https://github.com/linkedin/Liger-Kernel) optimizations. Increases
   325 |             multi-GPU throughput by ~20% and reduces memory usage by ~60%. Works with Flash Attention,
   326 |             FSDP, and DeepSpeed. Currently supports Llama, Mistral, Mixtral, and Gemma models.
   327 |         liger_kernel_config (`Optional[dict]`, *optional*):
   328 |             Configuration for Liger Kernel. Passed as kwargs to `_apply_liger_kernel_to_instance()`.
   329 |             Options typically include: `"rope"`, `"swiglu"`, `"cross_entropy"`,
   330 |             `"fused_linear_cross_entropy"`, `"rms_norm"`. If `None`, uses default configuration.
   331 | 
   332 |         > Additional Optimizations
   333 | 
   334 |         use_cache (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   335 |             Whether or not to enable cache for the model. For training, this is usually not needed apart from some PEFT methods that uses `past_key_values`.
   336 |         neftune_noise_alpha (`Optional[float]`):
   337 |             If not `None`, this will activate NEFTune noise embeddings. This can drastically improve model performance
   338 |             for instruction fine-tuning. Check out the [original paper](https://huggingface.co/papers/2310.05914) and the
   339 |             [original code](https://github.com/neelsjain/NEFTune). Support transformers `PreTrainedModel` and also
   340 |             `PeftModel` from peft. The original paper used values in the range [5.0, 15.0].
   341 |         torch_empty_cache_steps (`int`, *optional*):
   342 |             Number of steps to wait before calling `torch.<device>.empty_cache()`. If left unset or set to None, cache will not be emptied.
   343 |             This can help avoid CUDA out-of-memory errors by lowering peak VRAM usage at a cost of about [10% slower performance](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/issues/31372).
   344 |         auto_find_batch_size (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`)
   345 |             Whether to find a batch size that will fit into memory automatically through exponential decay, avoiding
   346 |             CUDA Out-of-Memory errors.
   347 | 
   348 |         > Logging & Monitoring Training
   349 | 
   350 |         logging_strategy (`str` or [`~trainer_utils.IntervalStrategy`], *optional*, defaults to `"steps"`):
   351 |             The logging strategy to adopt during training. Possible values are:
   352 |                 - `"no"`: No logging is done during training.
   353 |                 - `"epoch"`: Logging is done at the end of each epoch.
   354 |                 - `"steps"`: Logging is done every `logging_steps`.
   355 |         logging_steps (`int` or `float`, *optional*, defaults to 500):
   356 |             Number of update steps between two logs if `logging_strategy="steps"`. Should be an integer or a float in
   357 |             range `[0,1)`. If smaller than 1, will be interpreted as ratio of total training steps.
   358 |         logging_first_step (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   359 |             Whether to log the first `global_step` or not.
   360 |         log_on_each_node (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `True`):
   361 |             In multinode distributed training, whether to log using `log_level` once per node, or only on the main
   362 |             node.
   363 |         logging_nan_inf_filter (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `True`):
   364 |              Filter out NaN and Inf losses when logging. If `True`, replaces NaN/Inf losses with the
   365 |             average of recent valid losses. Does not affect gradient computation, only logging.
   366 |         include_num_input_tokens_seen (`Optional[Union[str, bool]]`, *optional*, defaults to "no"):
   367 |             Whether to track the number of input tokens seen. Must be one of ["all", "non_padding", "no"] or a boolean value which map to "all" or "no".
   368 |             May be slower in distributed training as gather operations must be called.
   369 | 
   370 |         > Logging
   371 | 
   372 |         log_level (`str`, *optional*, defaults to `passive`):
   373 |             Logging level for the main process. Options: `"debug"`, `"info"`, `"warning"`, `"error"`,
   374 |             `"critical"`, or `"passive"` (doesn't change the current Transformers logging level,
   375 |             which defaults to `"warning"`)
   376 |         log_level_replica (`str`, *optional*, defaults to `"warning"`):
   377 |             Logging level for replica processes in distributed training. Same options as `log_level`.
   378 |         disable_tqdm (`bool`, *optional*):
   379 |             Disable tqdm progress bars. Defaults to `True` if `log_level` is warning or lower, `False` otherwise.
   380 | 
   381 |         > Experiment Tracking Integration
   382 | 
   383 |         report_to (`str` or `list[str]`, *optional*, defaults to `"none"`):
   384 |             The list of integrations to report the results and logs to. Supported platforms are `"azure_ml"`,
   385 |             `"clearml"`, `"codecarbon"`, `"comet_ml"`, `"dagshub"`, `"dvclive"`, `"flyte"`, `"mlflow"`, `"swanlab"`,
   386 |             `"tensorboard"`, `"trackio"` and `"wandb"`. Use `"all"` to report to all integrations installed, `"none"`
   387 |             for no integrations.
   388 |         run_name (`str`, *optional*):
   389 |             A descriptor for the run. Typically used for [trackio](https://github.com/gradio-app/trackio),
   390 |             [wandb](https://www.wandb.com/), [mlflow](https://www.mlflow.org/), [comet](https://www.comet.com/site) and
   391 |             [swanlab](https://swanlab.cn) logging.
   392 |         project (`str`, *optional*, defaults to `"huggingface"`):
   393 |             The name of the project to use for logging. Currently, only used by Trackio.
   394 |         trackio_space_id (`str` or `None`, *optional*, defaults to `None`):
   395 |             The Hugging Face Space ID to use for live Trackio logging with a Gradio-based Space. Should be a full
   396 |             Space name like `'username/reponame'` or `'orgname/reponame'`, or just `'reponame'` (the Space is created in
   397 |             the currently logged-in user's namespace). If `None`, metrics are logged only to a **local** directory (no
   398 |             Space on the Hub). That Gradio Space has **read and write** access to the Trackio **Bucket**, which is what you want while
   399 |             training is **in progress**—for example when **resuming** a partial run or **aggregating logs** from multiple
   400 |             machines. The Space will be **public** unless you set `hub_private_repo=True` or your organization's default is to
   401 |             create private Spaces.
   402 |         trackio_bucket_id (`str` or `None`, *optional*, defaults to `None`):
   403 |             Optional Hugging Face Bucket id for Trackio. If unset, Trackio derives one. Used together with a Gradio Space
   404 |             (`trackio_space_id`) and when deploying a static Space (`trackio_static_space_id` is not `False`).
   405 |         trackio_static_space_id (`str`, `False`, or `None`, *optional*, defaults to `None`):
   406 |             The Hugging Face Space ID to use for static Space created after training is complete. Should be a full
   407 |             Space name like `'username/reponame'` or `'orgname/reponame'`, or just `'reponame'` (the Space is created in
   408 |             the currently logged-in user's namespace). If False, no static Space will be created. If None, and model is pushed to the Hub,
   409 |             a static Space will be created with a default name and this will be linked from the model card. The Space will be public
   410 |             unless you set `hub_private_repo=True` or your organization's default is to create private Spaces.
   411 | 
   412 |         > Evaluation
   413 | 
   414 |         eval_strategy (`str` or [`~trainer_utils.IntervalStrategy`], *optional*, defaults to `"no"`):
   415 |             When to run evaluation. Options:
   416 |             - `"no"`: No evaluation during training
   417 |             - `"steps"`: Evaluate every `eval_steps`
   418 |             - `"epoch"`: Evaluate at the end of each epoch
   419 |         eval_steps (`int` or `float`, *optional*):
   420 |             Number of update steps between two evaluations if `eval_strategy="steps"`. Will default to the same
   421 |             value as `logging_steps` if not set. Should be an integer or a float in range `[0,1)`. If smaller than 1,
   422 |             will be interpreted as ratio of total training steps.
   423 |         eval_delay (`float`, *optional*):
   424 |             Number of epochs or steps to wait for before the first evaluation can be performed, depending on the
   425 |             eval_strategy.
   426 |         per_device_eval_batch_size (`int`, *optional*, defaults to 8):
   427 |             The batch size per device accelerator core/CPU for evaluation.
   428 |         prediction_loss_only (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   429 |             When performing evaluation and generating predictions, only returns the loss.
   430 |         eval_on_start (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   431 |             Whether to perform a evaluation step (sanity check) before the training to ensure the validation steps works correctly.
   432 |         eval_do_concat_batches (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `True`):
   433 |             Whether to recursively concat inputs/losses/labels/predictions across batches. If `False`,
   434 |             will instead store them as lists, with each batch kept separate.
   435 |         eval_use_gather_object (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   436 |             Whether to run recursively gather object in a nested list/tuple/dictionary of objects from all devices. This should only be enabled if users are not just returning tensors, and this is actively discouraged by PyTorch.
   437 |             This is useful when the labels structure is non standard, like in computer vision tasks.
   438 |         eval_accumulation_steps (`int`, *optional*):
   439 |             Number of predictions steps to accumulate the output tensors for, before moving the results to the CPU. If
   440 |             left unset, the whole predictions are accumulated on the device accelerator before being moved to the CPU (faster but
   441 |             requires more memory).
   442 | 
   443 |         > Metrics Computation
   444 | 
   445 |         include_for_metrics (`list[str]`, *optional*, defaults to `[]`):
   446 |             Include additional data in the `compute_metrics` function if needed for metrics computation.
   447 |             Possible options to add to `include_for_metrics` list:
   448 |             - `"inputs"`: Input data passed to the model, intended for calculating input dependent metrics.
   449 |             - `"loss"`: Loss values computed during evaluation, intended for calculating loss dependent metrics.
   450 |         batch_eval_metrics (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   451 |             If set to `True`, evaluation will call compute_metrics at the end of each batch to accumulate statistics
   452 |             rather than saving all eval logits in memory. When set to `True`, you must pass a compute_metrics function
   453 |             that takes a boolean argument `compute_result`, which when passed `True`, will trigger the final global
   454 |             summary statistics from the batch-level summary statistics you've accumulated over the evaluation set.
   455 | 
   456 |         > Checkpointing & Saving
   457 | 
   458 |         save_only_model (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   459 |             Save only model weights, not optimizer/scheduler/RNG state. Significantly reduces
   460 |             checkpoint size but prevents resuming training from the checkpoint. Use when you only
   461 |             need the trained model for inference, not continued training.
   462 |             You can only load the model using `from_pretrained` with this option set to `True`.
   463 |         save_strategy (`str` or [`~trainer_utils.SaveStrategy`], *optional*, defaults to `"steps"`):
   464 |             The checkpoint save strategy to adopt during training. Possible values are:
   465 |                 - `"no"`: No save is done during training.
   466 |                 - `"epoch"`: Save is done at the end of each epoch.
   467 |                 - `"steps"`: Save is done every `save_steps`.
   468 |                 - `"best"`: Save is done whenever a new `best_metric` is achieved.
   469 |         save_steps (`int` or `float`, *optional*, defaults to 500):
   470 |             Number of updates steps before two checkpoint saves if `save_strategy="steps"`. Should be an integer or a
   471 |             float in range `[0,1)`. If smaller than 1, will be interpreted as ratio of total training steps.
   472 |         save_on_each_node (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   473 |             When doing multi-node distributed training, whether to save models and checkpoints on each node, or only on
   474 |             the main one.
   475 |             This should not be activated when the different nodes use the same storage as the files will be saved with
   476 |             the same names for each node.
   477 |         save_total_limit (`int`, *optional*):
   478 |             Maximum number of checkpoints to keep. Deletes older checkpoints in `output_dir`. When
   479 |             `load_best_model_at_end=True`, the best checkpoint is always retained plus the most
   480 |             recent ones. For example, `save_total_limit=5` keeps the 4 most recent plus the best
   481 |         enable_jit_checkpoint (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   482 |             Enable Just-In-Time checkpointing on SIGTERM signal for graceful termination on
   483 |             preemptible workloads. **Important**: Configure your orchestrator's graceful shutdown
   484 |             period to allow sufficient time. For Kubernetes, set `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`
   485 |             (default 30s is usually insufficient). For Slurm, use `--signal=TERM@<seconds>`.
   486 |             Required grace period ≥ longest iteration time + checkpoint save time.
   487 | 
   488 |         > Hugging Face Hub Integration
   489 | 
   490 |         push_to_hub (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   491 |             Whether or not to push the model to the Hub every time the model is saved. If this is activated,
   492 |             `output_dir` will begin a git directory synced with the repo (determined by `hub_model_id`) and the content
   493 |             will be pushed each time a save is triggered (depending on your `save_strategy`). Calling
   494 |             [`~Trainer.save_model`] will also trigger a push.
   495 |         hub_token (`str`, *optional*):
   496 |             The token to use to push the model to the Hub. Will default to the token in the cache folder obtained with
   497 |             `hf auth login`.
   498 |         hub_private_repo (`bool`, *optional*):
   499 |             Whether to make the repo private. If `None` (default), the repo will be public unless the organization's
   500 |             default is private. This value is ignored if the repo already exists. If reporting to Trackio with
   501 |             deployment to Hugging Face Spaces enabled, the same logic determines whether the Space is private.
   502 |         hub_model_id (`str`, *optional*):
   503 |             The name of the repository to keep in sync with the local *output_dir*. It can be a simple model ID in
   504 |             which case the model will be pushed in your namespace. Otherwise it should be the whole repository name,
   505 |             for instance `"user_name/model"`, which allows you to push to an organization you are a member of with
   506 |             `"organization_name/model"`. Will default to `user_name/output_dir_name` with *output_dir_name* being the
   507 |             name of `output_dir`.
   508 |         hub_strategy (`str` or [`~trainer_utils.HubStrategy`], *optional*, defaults to `"every_save"`):
   509 |             Defines what and when to push to Hub. Options:
   510 |                 - `"end"`: Push only at the end of training
   511 |                 - `"every_save"`: Push on each save (async to not block training)
   512 |                 - `"checkpoint"`: Like `"every_save"` plus push latest checkpoint to `"last-checkpoint"` subfolder for easy resuming
   513 |                 - `"all_checkpoints"`: Push all checkpoints as they appear
   514 |         hub_always_push (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   515 |             Unless this is `True`, the `Trainer` will skip pushing a checkpoint when the previous push is not finished.
   516 |         hub_revision (`str`, *optional*):
   517 |             The revision to use when pushing to the Hub. Can be a branch name, a tag, or a commit hash.
   518 | 
   519 |         > Best Model Tracking
   520 | 
   521 |         load_best_model_at_end (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   522 |             Load the best checkpoint at the end of training. Requires `eval_strategy` to be set.
   523 |             When enabled, the best checkpoint is always saved (see `save_total_limit`).
   524 |             <Tip>
   525 |             When `True`, `save_strategy` must match `eval_strategy` (unless `save_strategy` is `"best"`), and if using `"steps"`,
   526 |             `save_steps` must be a multiple of `eval_steps`.
   527 |             </Tip>
   528 |         metric_for_best_model (`str`, *optional*):
   529 |             Metric to use for comparing models when `load_best_model_at_end=True`. Must be a metric
   530 |             name returned by evaluation, with or without the `"eval_"` prefix. Defaults to `"loss"`.
   531 |             If you set this, `greater_is_better` will default to `True` unless the name ends with
   532 |             `"loss"`. Examples: `"accuracy"`, `"f1"`, `"eval_bleu"`.
   533 |         greater_is_better (`bool`, *optional*):
   534 |             Whether higher metric values are better. Defaults based on `metric_for_best_model`:
   535 |             `True` if the metric name doesn't end in `"loss"`, `False` otherwise.
   536 | 
   537 |         > Resuming Training
   538 | 
   539 |         ignore_data_skip (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   540 |             When resuming training, skip fast-forwarding through the dataset to reach the previous
   541 |             state. If `True`, training starts from the beginning of the dataset (faster resume but
   542 |             results won't match interrupted training). If `False`, skips seen data (slower resume
   543 |             but exact continuation).
   544 |         restore_callback_states_from_checkpoint (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   545 |             Restore callback states from checkpoint when resuming. If `True`, will override callbacks
   546 |             passed to Trainer if they exist in the checkpoint.
   547 | 
   548 |         > Reproducibility
   549 | 
   550 |         full_determinism (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`)
   551 |             If `True`, [`enable_full_determinism`] is called instead of [`set_seed`] to ensure reproducible results in
   552 |             distributed training. Important: this will negatively impact the performance, so only use it for debugging.
   553 |         seed (`int`, *optional*, defaults to 42):
   554 |             Random seed that will be set at the beginning of training. To ensure reproducibility across runs, use the
   555 |             [`~Trainer.model_init`] function to instantiate the model if it has some randomly initialized parameters.
   556 |         data_seed (`int`, *optional*):
   557 |             Random seed to be used with data samplers. If not set, random generators for data sampling will use the
   558 |             same seed as `seed`. This can be used to ensure reproducibility of data sampling, independent of the model
   559 |             seed.
   560 | 
   561 |         > Hardware Configuration
   562 | 
   563 |         use_cpu (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   564 |             Whether or not to use cpu. If set to False, we will use the available torch device/backend.
   565 | 
   566 |         > Accelerate Configuration
   567 | 
   568 |         accelerator_config (`str`, `dict`, or `AcceleratorConfig`, *optional*):
   569 |             Configuration for the internal Accelerate integration. Can be:
   570 |                 - Path to JSON config file: `"accelerator_config.json"`
   571 |                 - Dictionary with config options
   572 |                 - `AcceleratorConfig` instance
   573 |             Key options:
   574 |                 - `split_batches` (`bool`, defaults to `False`): Whether to split batches across devices.
   575 |                     If `True`, actual batch size is the same on all devices (total must be divisible by
   576 |                     num_processes). If `False`, each device gets the specified batch size.
   577 |                 - `dispatch_batches` (`bool`): If `True`, only main process iterates through dataloader
   578 |                     and dispatches batches to devices. Defaults to `True` for `IterableDataset`, `False`
   579 |                     otherwise.
   580 |                 - `even_batches` (`bool`, defaults to `True`): Duplicate samples from dataset start to
   581 |                     ensure all workers get equal batch sizes.
   582 |                 - `use_seedable_sampler` (`bool`, defaults to `True`): Use fully seedable random sampler
   583 |                     for reproducibility.
   584 |                 - `use_configured_state` (`bool`, defaults to `False`): Use pre-initialized
   585 |                     `AcceleratorState`/`PartialState` instead of creating new one. May cause issues with
   586 |                     hyperparameter tuning.
   587 | 
   588 |         parallelism_config (`ParallelismConfig`, *optional*):
   589 |             Parallelism configuration for the training run. Requires Accelerate `1.10.1`
   590 | 
   591 |         > Dataloader
   592 | 
   593 |         dataloader_drop_last (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   594 |             Whether to drop the last incomplete batch (if the length of the dataset is not divisible by the batch size)
   595 |             or not.
   596 |         dataloader_num_workers (`int`, *optional*, defaults to 0):
   597 |             Number of subprocesses to use for data loading (PyTorch only). 0 means that the data will be loaded in the
   598 |             main process.
   599 |         dataloader_pin_memory (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `True`):
   600 |             Whether you want to pin memory in data loaders or not. Will default to `True`.
   601 |         dataloader_persistent_workers (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   602 |             If True, the data loader will not shut down the worker processes after a dataset has been consumed once.
   603 |             This allows to maintain the workers Dataset instances alive. Can potentially speed up training, but will
   604 |             increase RAM usage. Will default to `False`.
   605 |         dataloader_prefetch_factor (`int`, *optional*):
   606 |             Number of batches loaded in advance by each worker.
   607 |             2 means there will be a total of 2 * num_workers batches prefetched across all workers.
   608 |         remove_unused_columns (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `True`):
   609 |             Whether or not to automatically remove the columns unused by the model forward method.
   610 |         label_names (`list[str]`, *optional*):
   611 |             The list of keys in your dictionary of inputs that correspond to the labels.
   612 |             Will eventually default to the list of argument names accepted by the model that contain the word "label",
   613 |             except if the model used is one of the `XxxForQuestionAnswering` in which case it will also include the
   614 |             `["start_positions", "end_positions"]` keys.
   615 |             You should only specify `label_names` if you're using custom label names or if your model's `forward` consumes multiple label tensors (e.g., extractive QA).
   616 |         train_sampling_strategy (`str`, *optional*, defaults to `"random"`):
   617 |             The sampler to use for the training dataloader. Possible values are:
   618 | 
   619 |                 - `"random"`: Uses `RandomSampler` (default).
   620 |                 - `"sequential"`: Uses `SequentialSampler`.
   621 |                 - `"group_by_length"`: Uses `LengthGroupedSampler` to group samples of roughly the same length
   622 |                   together (to minimize padding and be more efficient).
   623 | 
   624 |             Note: When using an `IterableDataset`, this argument is ignored.
   625 |         length_column_name (`str`, *optional*, defaults to `"length"`):
   626 |             Column name for precomputed lengths. If the column exists, grouping by length will use these values rather
   627 |             than computing them on train startup. Ignored unless `train_sampling_strategy` is `"group_by_length"` and the dataset
   628 |             is an instance of `Dataset`.
   629 | 
   630 |         > DDP (DistributedDataParallel)
   631 | 
   632 |         ddp_find_unused_parameters (`bool`, *optional*):
   633 |             When using distributed training, the value of the flag `find_unused_parameters` passed to
   634 |             `DistributedDataParallel`. Will default to `False` if gradient checkpointing is used, `True` otherwise.
   635 |         ddp_bucket_cap_mb (`int`, *optional*):
   636 |             When using distributed training, the value of the flag `bucket_cap_mb` passed to `DistributedDataParallel`.
   637 |         ddp_broadcast_buffers (`bool`, *optional*):
   638 |             When using distributed training, the value of the flag `broadcast_buffers` passed to
   639 |             `DistributedDataParallel`. Will default to `False` if gradient checkpointing is used, `True` otherwise.
   640 |         ddp_static_graph (`bool`, *optional*):
   641 |             When using distributed training, the value of the flag `static_graph` passed to
   642 |             `DistributedDataParallel`.
   643 |         ddp_backend (`str`, *optional*):
   644 |             The backend to use for distributed training. Must be one of `"nccl"`, `"mpi"`, `"xccl"`, `"gloo"`, `"hccl"`.
   645 |         ddp_timeout (`int`, *optional*, defaults to 1800):
   646 |             The timeout for `torch.distributed.init_process_group` calls, used to avoid GPU socket timeouts when
   647 |             performing slow operations in distributed runnings. Please refer to the [PyTorch documentation](https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/distributed.html#torch.distributed.init_process_group) for more
   648 |             information.
   649 | 
   650 |         > FSDP (Fully Sharded Data Parallel)
   651 | 
   652 |         fsdp (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `None`):
   653 |             Enable PyTorch Fully Sharded Data Parallel (FSDP) for distributed training. Pass `True` to enable FSDP.
   654 |         fsdp_config (`str` or `dict`, *optional*):
   655 |             Configuration settings for when `fsdp` is enabled. Pass a path to a JSON config file, such
   656 |             as `fsdp_config.json`, or an already-loaded dict.
   657 | 
   658 |             Supported keys:
   659 |                 - version (`int`, *optional*, defaults to `2`):
   660 |                     The version of FSDP to use (`2` for FSDP2, `1` for the legacy FSDP1).
   661 |                 - reshard_after_forward (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `True`):
   662 |                     Whether to reshard parameters after the forward pass. Set to `False` to keep parameters
   663 |                     gathered between the forward and backward passes, avoids the re-all-gather, and use higher peak memory.
   664 |                 - cpu_offload (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   665 |                     Offload parameters and gradients to CPU when not in use to save GPU memory.
   666 |                 - activation_checkpointing (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   667 |                     Set to `True` to reduce memory by recomputing activations during the backward pass. Prefer
   668 |                     `activation_checkpointing` over `gradient_checkpointing` when using FSDP. `gradient_checkpointing`
   669 |                     introduces a redundant all-gather in the backward pass.
   670 |                 - cpu_ram_efficient_loading (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   671 |                     Set to `True` to load the pretrained checkpoint on the first process only. Other processes start
   672 |                     with empty weights and receive the weights by broadcast.
   673 |                 - state_dict_type (`str`, *optional*, defaults to `"FULL_STATE_DICT"`):
   674 |                     Checkpoint format: `"FULL_STATE_DICT"` (single HF-compatible file) or
   675 |                     `"SHARDED_STATE_DICT"` (one file per rank, faster for large models).
   676 |                 - auto_wrap_policy (`str`, *optional*, defaults to `"TRANSFORMER_BASED_WRAP"`):
   677 |                     Auto-wrap policy to use. Choose `"TRANSFORMER_BASED_WRAP"`, `"SIZE_BASED_WRAP"`, or `"NO_WRAP"`.
   678 |                 - transformer_layer_cls_to_wrap (`list[str]`, *optional*):
   679 |                     Transformer layer class names (case-sensitive) to wrap, e.g. `LlamaDecoderLayer`. Usually
   680 |                     unnecessary: the wrap policy falls back to the model's `_no_split_modules`, which covers
   681 |                     most transformers models.
   682 |                 - min_num_params (`int`, *optional*, defaults to `0`):
   683 |                     Minimum number of parameters per module for size-based auto-wrapping (used with
   684 |                     `auto_wrap_policy="SIZE_BASED_WRAP"`).
   685 |                 - xla (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   686 |                     Whether to use PyTorch/XLA Fully Sharded Data Parallel Training. Experimental.
   687 |                 - xla_fsdp_settings (`dict`, *optional*):
   688 |                     Dictionary of XLA FSDP wrapping parameters. For a complete list of options, see the
   689 |                     [XLA FSDP source](https://github.com/pytorch/xla/blob/master/torch_xla/distributed/fsdp/xla_fully_sharded_data_parallel.py).
   690 |                 - xla_fsdp_grad_ckpt (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   691 |                     Set to `True` to use gradient checkpointing over each nested XLA FSDP wrapped layer. Requires
   692 |                     `xla=True` and an auto-wrapping policy (`min_num_params` or `transformer_layer_cls_to_wrap`).
   693 | 
   694 |         > DeepSpeed
   695 | 
   696 |         deepspeed (`str` or `dict`, *optional*):
   697 |              Enable [DeepSpeed](https://github.com/deepspeedai/DeepSpeed) integration. Value is either:
   698 |                 - Path to DeepSpeed JSON config file: `"ds_config.json"`
   699 |                 - Loaded config as dictionary
   700 |           > [!TIP]
   701 |           > If using ZeRO initialization, instantiate your model *after* initializing
   702 |           `TrainingArguments`, otherwise ZeRO won't be applied.
   703 | 
   704 |         > Debugging & Profiling (Experimental)
   705 | 
   706 |         debug (`str` or list of [`~debug_utils.DebugOption`], *optional*, defaults to `""`):
   707 |             Enable one or more debug features. This is an experimental feature.
   708 |             Possible options are:
   709 |             - "underflow_overflow": detects overflow in model's input/outputs and reports the last frames that led to
   710 |               the event
   711 |             - "tpu_metrics_debug": print debug metrics on TPU
   712 |         skip_memory_metrics (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `True`):
   713 |             Whether to skip adding of memory profiler reports to metrics. This is skipped by default because it slows
   714 |             down the training and evaluation speed.
   715 | 
   716 |         > External Script Flags (not used by Trainer)
   717 | 
   718 |         do_train (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   719 |             Whether to run training or not. This argument is not directly used by [`Trainer`], it's intended to be used
   720 |             by your training/evaluation scripts instead. See the [example
   721 |             scripts](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/tree/main/examples) for more details.
   722 |         do_eval (`bool`, *optional*):
   723 |             Whether to run evaluation on the validation set or not. Will be set to `True` if `eval_strategy` is
   724 |             different from `"no"`. This argument is not directly used by [`Trainer`], it's intended to be used by your
   725 |             training/evaluation scripts instead. See the [example
   726 |             scripts](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/tree/main/examples) for more details.
   727 |         do_predict (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `False`):
   728 |             Whether to run predictions on the test set or not. This argument is not directly used by [`Trainer`], it's
   729 |             intended to be used by your training/evaluation scripts instead. See the [example
   730 |             scripts](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/tree/main/examples) for more details.
   731 |         resume_from_checkpoint (`str`, *optional*):
   732 |             The path to a folder with a valid checkpoint for your model. This argument is not directly used by
   733 |             [`Trainer`], it's intended to be used by your training/evaluation scripts instead. See the [example
   734 |             scripts](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/tree/main/examples) for more details.
   735 |     """
