  2409 |                 `nan` or `inf` is filtered and the average loss of the current logging window is taken instead.
  2410 | 
  2411 |                 <Tip>
  2412 | 
  2413 |                 `nan_inf_filter` only influences the logging of loss values, it does not change the behavior the
  2414 |                 gradient is computed or applied to the model.
  2415 | 
  2416 |                 </Tip>
  2417 | 
  2418 |             on_each_node (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `True`):
  2419 |                 In multinode distributed training, whether to log using `log_level` once per node, or only on the main
  2420 |                 node.
  2421 |             replica_level (`str`, *optional*, defaults to `"passive"`):
  2422 |                 Logger log level to use on replicas. Same choices as `log_level`
  2423 | 
  2424 |         Example:
  2425 | 
  2426 |         ```py
  2427 |         >>> from transformers import TrainingArguments
  2428 | 
  2429 |         >>> args = TrainingArguments("working_dir")
  2430 |         >>> args = args.set_logging(strategy="steps", steps=100)
  2431 |         >>> args.logging_steps
  2432 |         100
  2433 |         ```
  2434 |         """
  2435 |         self.logging_strategy = IntervalStrategy(strategy)
  2436 |         if self.logging_strategy == IntervalStrategy.STEPS and steps == 0:
  2437 |             raise ValueError("Setting `strategy` as 'steps' requires a positive value for `steps`.")
  2438 |         self.logging_steps = steps
  2439 |         self.report_to = report_to
  2440 |         self.log_level = level
  2441 |         self.logging_first_step = first_step
  2442 |         self.logging_nan_inf_filter = nan_inf_filter
  2443 |         self.log_on_each_node = on_each_node
  2444 |         self.log_level_replica = replica_level
  2445 |         return self
