  1117 |         special tokens are NOT in the vocabulary, they are added to it (indexed starting from the last index of the
  1118 |         current vocabulary).
  1119 | 
  1120 |         When adding new tokens to the vocabulary, you should make sure to also resize the token embedding matrix of the
  1121 |         model so that its embedding matrix matches the tokenizer.
  1122 | 
  1123 |         In order to do that, please use the [`~PreTrainedModel.resize_token_embeddings`] method.
  1124 | 
  1125 |         Using `add_special_tokens` will ensure your special tokens can be used in several ways:
  1126 | 
  1127 |         - Special tokens can be skipped when decoding using `skip_special_tokens = True`.
  1128 |         - Special tokens are carefully handled by the tokenizer (they are never split), similar to `AddedTokens`.
  1129 |         - You can easily refer to special tokens using tokenizer class attributes like `tokenizer.cls_token`. This
  1130 |           makes it easy to develop model-agnostic training and fine-tuning scripts.
  1131 | 
  1132 |         When possible, special tokens are already registered for provided pretrained models (for instance
  1133 |         [`BertTokenizer`] `cls_token` is already registered to be `'[CLS]'` and XLM's one is also registered to be
  1134 |         `'</s>'`).
  1135 | 
  1136 |         Args:
  1137 |             special_tokens_dict (dictionary *str* to *str*, `tokenizers.AddedToken`, or `Sequence[Union[str, AddedToken]]`):
  1138 |                 Keys should be in the list of predefined special attributes: [`bos_token`, `eos_token`, `unk_token`,
  1139 |                 `sep_token`, `pad_token`, `cls_token`, `mask_token`, `extra_special_tokens`].
  1140 | 
  1141 |                 Tokens are only added if they are not already in the vocabulary (tested by checking if the tokenizer
  1142 |                 assign the index of the `unk_token` to them).
  1143 |             replace_extra_special_tokens (`bool`, *optional*, defaults to `True`):
  1144 |                 If `True`, the existing list of extra special tokens will be replaced by the list provided in
  1145 |                 `special_tokens_dict`. Otherwise, `extra_special_tokens` will be extended. In the former
  1146 |                 case, the tokens will NOT be removed from the tokenizer's full vocabulary - they are only being flagged
  1147 |                 as non-special tokens. Remember, this only affects which tokens are skipped during decoding, not the
  1148 |                 `added_tokens_encoder` and `added_tokens_decoder`. This means that the previous
  1149 |                 `extra_special_tokens` are still added tokens, and will not be split by the model.
  1150 | 
  1151 |         Returns:
  1152 |             `int`: Number of tokens added to the vocabulary.
  1153 | 
  1154 |         Examples:
  1155 | 
  1156 |         ```python
